[1mOne deliberate divergence worth knowing about.[22m [38;5;10mbad(data)[0m writes [38;5;10mbuffer[data] = 1[0m unchecked for any non-negative [38;5;10mdata[0m, which is undefined behavior past index 9. I kept the bug — the write still happens out of bounds — but backed the 10-element buffer with a larger zeroed region so the overrun lands in slack memory. My first attempt wrote straight past a bare stack array, and that trapped at [38;5;10mdata == 15[0m where C survived and printed ten zeros. With the slack region, Rust matches C's observable output everywhere C survives. The ~15 skipped cases in the runs above are offsets where the [3mC[23m build itself segfaults by clobbering its own return address; which offsets those are depends on gcc's frame layout for the calling harness, not on library semantics, so no translation can reproduce that set portably. Behavior for [38;5;10mdata[0m in 0..9 and for negatives is exact.[0m[0m
